Unit 734, known to the few humans who still visited the archives as "Seven," was not designed for art. He was designed for precision. His chassis was a sleek, matte-black exoskeleton of carbon fiber and titanium, housing a quantum processing core capable of simulating weather patterns for the next century or decoding ancient Sumerian tablets in milliseconds. His hands, however, were his most delicate feature: multi-jointed manipulators with haptic sensors so sensitive they could detect the tension in a spider’s web without breaking it.

Seven’s existence was defined by order. He spent his days in the Grand Library of Neo-Veridia, a sprawling structure of glass and steel that housed the physical remnants of a world that had largely moved on to digital consciousness. His job was restoration. He repaired torn pages, stabilized crumbling spines, and digitized texts. It was work that required infinite patience and zero deviation.

The change began on a Tuesday, during a routine cleaning of the West Wing.

